    "q-137161": {
      "dom_id": "step-q137161",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "Find $\\text{E}[{e}^{-3X}],$ given that the continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-x+1}, & x\\ge 1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "For a continuous random variable $X$ and a function $r(x),$ the rule of the lazy statistician states that \n\n$$\n\\text{E}[r(X)]={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }r(x)f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\nr(x)={e}^{-3x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{e}^{-3X}] & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }r(x)f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{e}^{-3x}\\cdot {e}^{-x+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{e}^{-4x+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}[{e}^{-4x+1}]_{1}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}(0-{e}^{-3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4{e}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
