    "q-111892": {
      "dom_id": "step-q111892",
      "name": "Question 7",
      "problem": "Find $\\text{E}[{X}^{3}-2X],$ given that the continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9},\\,0\\le x\\le 3.\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "For a continuous random variable $X$ and a function $r(x),$ the rule of the lazy statistician states that \n\n$$\n\\text{E}[r(X)]={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }r(x)f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\nr(x)={x}^{3}-2x.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{3}-2X] & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }r(x)f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}({x}^{3}-2x)\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(\\frac{{x}^{5}}{9}-\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{9})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{6}}{54}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{18}]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =[\\frac{27}{2}-\\frac{9}{2}]-0 \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
