    "q-149671": {
      "dom_id": "step-q149671",
      "name": "Question 9",
      "problem": "Find $\\text{E}[\\frac{13X}{1-X}],$ given that the continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{12}{13}(1-x)^{2},\\,-\\frac{1}{2}\\le x\\le \\frac{1}{2}.\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "For a continuous random variable $X$ and a function $r(x),$ the rule of the lazy statistician states that \n\n$$\n\\text{E}[r(X)]={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }r(x)f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\nr(x)=\\frac{13x}{1-x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[\\frac{13X}{1-X}] & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }r(x)f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}\\frac{13x}{1-x}\\cdot \\frac{12}{13}(1-x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}12x(1-x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =12{\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}(x-{x}^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =12{[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}]}_{-1/2}^{1/2} \\\\  & =12[(\\frac{1}{8}-\\frac{1}{24})-(\\frac{1}{8}+\\frac{1}{24})] \\\\  & =12(-\\frac{1}{12}) \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
