    "q-275732": {
      "dom_id": "step-q275732",
      "name": "Question 11",
      "problem": "A point is chosen uniformly at random from the interval $[0,1].$ Let the continuous random variable $X$ be the distance from the origin to the chosen point. Consider the triangle with vertices at $(0,0),(2,0),$ and $(2,X).$ Let $A=A(X)$ denote the area of this triangle.\n\nExpressing the integrand in the following equation as a polynomial, we have \n\n$E[A]={\\int }_{0}^{1}$\n{{field-1}} $dx$\n\nTherefore, the expected area of the triangle is \n{{field-2}}.",
      "worked_solution": "For a continuous random variable $X$ and a function $r(x)$, the rule of the lazy statistician states that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[r(x)]={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }r(x)f(x)\\text{ }\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)$ is the probability density function of $X.$ \n\nIn this problem, $X$ is chosen uniformly from $[0,1]$, so the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x)=1,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,0\\le x<1.\n$$\n\nLet $A$ denote the area of the triangle formed by the three points $(0,0),(2,0),$ and $(2,X).$ The triangle has a fixed base of length $2$ along the $x$-axis, and a height of length $X$ from $(2,0)$ to $(2,X).$ Using the standard formula for the area of a triangle, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\text{base}\\cdot \\text{height} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot X \\\\  & =X.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, we define\n\n$$\nr(x)=x.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the rule of the lazy statistician:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[A] & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }r(x)f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
