    "q-275751": {
      "dom_id": "step-q275751",
      "name": "Question 14",
      "problem": "A stick of length $6$ is broken at a point chosen uniformly at random along its length. Let the continuous random variable $X$ denote the distance from the left end of the stick to the break point, and let $Y=Y(X)$ denote the length of the shortest piece.\n\nExpressing the integrands in the following equation as polynomials, we have\n\n$\\text{E}[Y]={\\int }_{0}^{3}$\n{{field-1}} $\\text{d}x\\,\\,+\\,\\,{\\int }_{3}^{6}$\n{{field-2}} $\\text{d}x$\n\nTherefore, the expected length of the shorter piece is  \n{{field-3}}.",
      "worked_solution": "For a continuous random variable $X$ and a function $r(x)$, the rule of the lazy statistician states that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[r(x)]={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }r(x)f(x)\\text{ }\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)$ is the probability density function of $X.$ \n\nIn this problem, the break point $X$ is chosen uniformly from $[0,6]$, so the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{6},\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,0\\le x<6.\n$$\n\nWhen the stick is broken at point $X$, it is divided into two pieces: one of length $X$ and the other of length $6-X.$ The shorter of the two pieces, $Y$, is therefore given by\n\n$$\nY=\\text{min}(X,6-X).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we define\n\n$$\nr(x)=\\text{min}(x,6-x).\n$$\n\nTo evaluate the integral, we express $r(x)$ as an explicit piecewise function. Observe that,\n\n- if $0\\le x<3$, then $x<6-x$, so the shorter piece has length $x$,\n\n- if $3\\le x\\le 6$, then $x\\ge 6-x$, so the shorter piece has length $6-x.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nr(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}x, & 0\\le x<3, \\\\ 6-x, & 3\\le x\\le 6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now apply the rule of the lazy statistician:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & =\\text{E}[\\text{min}(X,6-X)] \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }r(x)f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}x\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{3}^{6}(6-x)\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{3}x\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{1}{6}{\\int }_{3}^{6}(6-x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}{[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{3}+\\frac{1}{6}{[6x-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{3}^{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(\\frac{9}{2})+\\frac{1}{6}[(36-18)-(18-\\frac{9}{2})] \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{1}{6}(18-\\frac{27}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{1}{6}(\\frac{9}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{3}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the expected length of the shorter piece is $\\frac{3}{2}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
